    "case_id": "amb1-case04-research-tool-release",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 1 USER]\ne001\tOur small academic software group has built an open tool for exploring archival text collections.\ne002\tIt is not used for medical or individual decisions; it helps researchers find patterns and passages to inspect.\ne003\tTwo labs have used private builds and want us to release a public beta.\ne004\tThe analysis engine is stable on our test collections, but installation is awkward and the documentation assumes people understand our data format.\ne005\tHalf the team wants feedback now.\ne006\tThe maintainers worry a messy release will define our reputation.\n[Turn 1 ASSISTANT]\ne007\tThe decision depends on what you want the beta to learn and which failures would be informative rather than merely predictable.\ne008\tIf most early users will fail at installation or formatting, the feedback may tell you what you already know and consume goodwill.\ne009\tIf the private labs have only used familiar collections, a broader beta could reveal important assumptions.\ne010\tYou might delay “public” while expanding a structured preview to users with different data.\n[Turn 2 USER]\ne011\tThat is close to what the maintainers say, but the structured preview has been “one more cohort” for six months.\ne012\tEach time we add users, we find another thing to document before going public.\ne013\tMeanwhile two volunteer contributors have stopped attending meetings because there is no visible release.\ne014\tI do not think contributor momentum is superficial.\ne015\tA tool that is always almost ready can become a private service for our friends.\n[Turn 2 ASSISTANT]\ne016\tAgreed—indefinite preview is itself a product choice, and it can narrow both the community and the evidence you receive.\ne017\tA public beta can create a focal point for contributors if its limits are explicit.\ne018\tThe maintainers' concern still matters because public access changes the volume and variety of support.\ne019\tThe question may be less “is it ready?”\ne020\tthan “what support promise accompanies the release, and can the team sustain that promise?”\n[Turn 3 USER]\ne021\tI am not sure disclaimers solve it.\ne022\tResearchers often ignore “beta” once they have invested in a workflow.\ne023\tIf we change the data model later, they may feel we broke something even if we warned them.\ne024\tAlso, the two labs want to cite the tool in papers this year.\ne025\tA public version would make that easier and may help our grant renewal, but I do not want citation pressure to freeze an interface we chose for convenience.\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne026\tThen the most consequential commitment may be the data and output format, not the release label.\ne027\tYou could mark some interfaces as experimental while stabilizing a minimal exchange format for saved projects.\ne028\tBut that creates engineering work and may imply more compatibility than you can guarantee.\ne029\tThe publication and grant benefits are real organizational incentives; they should be named openly rather than translated into an argument that users necessarily need the current interface now.",
